</style>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/4.7.0/css/font-awesome.min.css">
</head>
<body class="article toc2 toc-right">
<div id="header">
<h1>Adding more apps to a site</h1>
<div id="toc" class="toc2">
<div id="toctitle">Table of Contents</div>
<ul class="sectlevel1">
<li><a href="#more_than_one_app">More than one app</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_install_the_app">Task: Install the app</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_add_the_app_to_the_site">Task: Add the app to the site</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_publish_and_verify">Task: Publish and verify</a></li>
<li><a href="#how_the_app_takes_part">How the app takes part</a></li>
<li><a href="#more_apps_to_explore">More apps to explore</a></li>
<li><a href="#summary">Summary</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="content">
<div id="preamble">
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>In this chapter, you&#8217;ll install the Sitemap.xml app from Enonic Market, add it to your site, and see how an app can contribute to a site without rendering any of its pages.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>This chapter requires the result of <a href="../go-online.ahtml">chapter 9</a>, as described in <a href="../advanced.ahtml">Advanced features</a>. It refers to the <a href="mappings.ahtml">Mappings</a> chapter, but you don&#8217;t need to have completed it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="more_than_one_app"><a class="link" href="#more_than_one_app">More than one app</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Your app renders the site: it owns the pages, parts, and layouts. That job is best left to a single app.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Other apps can still be added to the same site, and add features of their own next to yours. A sitemap is a good example. It has nothing to do with how your pages look, every site needs one, and there is an app for it already.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="task_install_the_app"><a class="link" href="#task_install_the_app">Task: Install the app</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Open Applications</strong> - On the XP admin home page, click <b class="button">Applications</b>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Find the app</strong> - Click <b class="button">Install</b>, and type <code>Sitemap</code> in the search field:</p>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="../media/install-sitemap.png" alt="The Install Application dialog with Sitemap in the search field, listing the Sitemap.xml app" width="1440">
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Install the app</strong> - Click <b class="button">Install</b> next to <code>Sitemap.xml</code>, and close the dialog when it says <code>Installed</code>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Verify the result</strong> - The app is listed among the installed applications, as <code>Sitemap XML</code>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="task_add_the_app_to_the_site"><a class="link" href="#task_add_the_app_to_the_site">Task: Add the app to the site</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Open the site</strong> - In Content Studio, select <code>Hello World</code> and click <b class="button">Edit</b>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Add the app</strong> - In the <code>Applications</code> field, search for <code>Sitemap XML</code>, select it, and click <b class="button">Apply</b>. The app is listed below your own, <code>Site Framework tutorial</code>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Open the configuration</strong> - Click the pencil icon next to <code>Sitemap XML</code>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Include the countries</strong> - Below <code>Include by type</code>, click <b class="button">Add</b>. Select <code>Country</code> in the <code>Content Type</code> field, and click <b class="button">Apply</b>:</p>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="../media/sitemap-config.png" alt="The configuration of the Sitemap XML app, with Country selected in the Content Type field" width="1440">
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Content Studio saves the change automatically.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The configuration form is part of the Sitemap.xml app. An app can define a form, which Content Studio shows for every site the app is added to. The values are stored on the site.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="task_publish_and_verify"><a class="link" href="#task_publish_and_verify">Task: Publish and verify</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The list of apps and their configuration belong to the site content, so the change must be published before visitors get the sitemap.</p>
</div>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Publish the site</strong> - Go back to the content list, select <code>Hello World</code>, and click <b class="button">Mark as ready</b>. The Publishing Wizard opens. Click <b class="button">Publish now</b>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Verify the result</strong> - Open <a href="http://localhost:8080/site/siteframework/master/hello-world/sitemap.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">http://localhost:8080/site/siteframework/master/hello-world/sitemap.xml</a> in your browser. It lists the site and the countries:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-xml" data-lang="xml">&lt;urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9"&gt;
  &lt;url&gt;
    &lt;loc&gt;http://localhost:8080/site/siteframework/master/hello-world&lt;/loc&gt;
    ...
  &lt;/url&gt;
  &lt;url&gt;
    &lt;loc&gt;http://localhost:8080/site/siteframework/master/hello-world/norway&lt;/loc&gt;
    ...
  &lt;/url&gt;
  ...
&lt;/urlset&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="how_the_app_takes_part"><a class="link" href="#how_the_app_takes_part">How the app takes part</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You did not change your own app, and it knows nothing about the sitemap. The whole connection is in the site descriptor of the Sitemap.xml app:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">cms/site.yaml in the Sitemap.xml app</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">kind: "Site"
mappings:
  - controller: "/cms/controllers/sitemap/sitemap.js"
    order: 12
    pattern: "/sitemap(-\\d+)?\\.xml(\\.gz)?"</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>This is a mapping, like the one for <code>llms.txt</code> in the <a href="mappings.ahtml">Mappings</a> chapter. When a request for your site arrives, the site service collects the mappings of every app that is added to the site, and checks them before it renders anything:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p>A request for <code>sitemap.xml</code> matches the mapping in the Sitemap.xml app, and is answered there.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>A request for <code>llms.txt</code> matches the mapping in your app, if you have added it.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All other requests match no mapping, and continue to the page rendering in your app.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>order</code> decides which mapping wins when several of them match the same request. Lower numbers come first, and the default is 50.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Mappings are one of several ways for an app to take part in a site. An app can also run <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/code/stable/web/filters">filters</a> around the requests, change the rendered pages with <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/code/stable/web/sites/processors">response processors</a>, and offer <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/code/stable/web/apis">APIs</a> for other apps to mount. In all of these cases the rule stays the same: one app renders the site, and the others contribute around it.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
The <a href="../demo.ahtml">demo</a> has the Sitemap.xml app added to the site already. Its sandbox is created from the <code>sitekit</code> template, which installs the app.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="more_apps_to_explore"><a class="link" href="#more_apps_to_explore">More apps to explore</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Sitemap.xml is one of many apps that contribute to a site in this way. These are worth a look:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p><a href="https://market.enonic.com/vendors/enonic/com.enonic.app.robotstxt">Robots.txt</a> - Serves <code>robots.txt</code> for your site, editable in Content Studio</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><a href="https://market.enonic.com/vendors/enonic/com.enonic.app.metafields">SEO Meta Fields</a> - Adds fields for search engine optimization to your content, and the matching meta tags to your pages</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><a href="https://market.enonic.com/vendors/enonic/booster">Booster</a> - Caches the responses of your site to improve performance. Booster requires an Enonic license</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You install them and add them to your site the same way as Sitemap.xml. Visit <a href="https://market.enonic.com">Enonic Market</a> for the full selection.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="summary"><a class="link" href="#summary">Summary</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You&#8217;ve installed an app from Enonic Market, added it to your site, and seen how its mapping works side by side with the rendering in your own app.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>That completes the advanced features.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="footer">
<div id="footer-text">
Last updated 2026-11-25 08:43:48 UTC
</div>
</div>
</body>
</html>